  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 202 PM EST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period on Friday, a digging shortwave \nwill be in the process of phasing with a broader closed low situated \nacross the southern Great Lakes. As the aforementioned low shifts to \nthe south and east, a secondary (likely dry) cold front will trek \nacross north and central Georgia -- sending a reinforcing push of \nsupremely dry air across the Southeast. Isolated to scattered \nshowers and embedded thunderstorms may be ongoing across the eastern \nportion of the forecast area around daybreak through the early \nafternoon Friday, forced by what remains of the front supporting \nfairly widespread showers at the time of writing of this AFD. PWATs \nwill drop swiftly behind the second frontal push, with much of the \nSoutheast progged to sit at values of 0.5\" or lower through the \nweekend (generally between the 10th and 25th percentile \nclimatologically). Rain chances will be similarly effaced, likely \nremaining 10% or less through midweek next week as a regime of dry, \nnorthwesterly flow sets up in the midlevels. Fairly significant \nmodel discrepancies arise from Tuesday on -- the GFS favors a mature \nlow pressure system and expansive precipitation shield riding along \nthe northern Gulf, while the Euro lifts a much weaker shortwave \nacross the southern Plains, fizzling out over the Tennessee River \nValley. This would be the difference between a breezy, overrunning \ndeluge of rainfall and perhaps not a drop of rain, so care will need \nto be taken to monitor trends over the next several model runs. \n\nAfternoon highs initially in the 70s to lower 80s on Friday will \nbecome more seasonable into the weekend, in the 60s to lower 70s. \nMorning lows will return to the 30s to mid-40s on Sunday morning, \nand potentially in the upper 20s for portions of northeast Georgia \nat elevation. We are in the heart of frost/freeze season, so \nsubsequent product issuance may be necessary for portions of far \nnorth Georgia on Sunday and Monday night/morning.",
